
  [1mB. ES modules.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m; [38;5;153msettings.js[39m does [38;5;153mexport const settings[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  does [38;5;153mimport { settings } from './settings.js'[39m. This is an actual module boundary — the dependency is written in the
  file that has it, there's no global, and the resolver function can be imported and tested directly. One hard cost:
  [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is fetched under CORS rules, so [1mthe page stops working from [38;5;153mfile://[39m and must be served over http[0m. That's
  a breaking change if you currently open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it.

  [1mC. Dual-format (UMD-style) settings.[0m [38;5;153msettings.js[39m detects [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m and exposes both a CommonJS export and a
  browser global, so Node can require it while the browser gets a global. Testable without a browser and still
  [38;5;153mfile://[39m-safe. I'd discard this one: it's boilerplate serving a test runner this repo doesn't have, and it adds a third
  module convention to a project that already has two. YAGNI.

  [1mRecommendation: B, conditional on one fact I don't have.[0m B is what "a settings module" properly means and it's the
  only option where the dependency is explicit, but it's the wrong call if you open this page from the filesystem rather
  than through a server. If you do, take A — it's boring, but it cannot break, and the global is a small price in a
  project this size.

  Two things I'll fold into the design either way:

  - [1mUnknown hostname should fail loudly, not guess.[0m Silently defaulting to production means a misconfigured host posts
    credentials to your real backend. I'd throw an explicit error naming the unrecognized hostname.
  - [1m[38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is currently only mentioned in a comment[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never uses it. So the move is
    mechanically trivial; the design work is entirely in the loading and resolution structure.
